WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.445
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:04.401
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.401 --> 00:00:05.868
[CLICKING]

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.000
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.330
So we are going to start a new
chapter in this class, Feynman

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.010
Calculus.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.260
The purpose of this
very first introduction

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.750
is to just set the stage.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.670
And we do this by
recapping what we've

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.920
discussed so far in the first
three weeks of this course.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.410
So first we
introduced the player

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.794
in the field-- the elementary
particles, the matter

00:00:32.794 --> 00:00:35.300
particles, and the
force carriers.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.120
We have seen that there are
three generation of fermions,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.430
that there's leptons and quarks.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.070
We have seen that
they, eh, in principle,

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.760
interact in different ways
with those force carriers.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.890
We have seen that there
are three kinds of charges,

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.890
or three kinds of interactions--

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.920
the electromagnetic interaction,
the weak interaction,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.800
and the strong interaction.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.360
Then we moved on and had
a quantitative discussion

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.500
of relativistic kinematics.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.600
So there's quite a set of
useful problems we can look at.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.860
For example, we are
wondering, how much energy

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.010
does my proton need in a
beam on a fixed target where

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.550
we want to produce antiprotons?

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.230
So those problems we were able
to discuss and quantitatively

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:21.980
figure out what the answers
to those questions are.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.870
Then we looked at
Feynman diagram.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.390
We are able to read
them and understand

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.450
what they principally mean.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.210
And then we had a discussion
last week on symmetries.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.930
And we introduced parity, we
introduced charge conjugation,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.680
we looked at CP
and CP violation.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.310
We had a qualitative discussion
on decays and scattering.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.730
We defined what a
geometrical cross-section is.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.550
And now we are really starting
the quantitative discussion

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.140
of particle dynamics.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.960
We do this now in the
next video by introducing

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.540
Fermi's golden rule.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:00.770
And then we study
a toy theory which

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.280
is simplified such that
the algebra involved is not

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.180
going to be too
much of a hassle,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.430
so we can focus on understanding
the following Feynman

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.800
rules in order to
calculate decay rates

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.880
and scattering cross-sections.

